  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "Amongst the following compounds, the one which would not respond to iodoform test is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "YxetX",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}(\\mathrm{OH}) \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "VHrHA",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{ICH}_{2} \\mathrm{COCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "znsSf",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "jIZfb",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Acetic acid, \\(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}\\) would not respond to iodoform test. In the mechanism of haloform reaction, in the first step, base deprotonates most acidic alpha \\(\\mathrm{H}\\) atom (of methyl keto group).\r\nIn acetic acid, the most acidic proton is attached to \\(\\mathrm{O}\\) atom. So deprotonation of \\(\\alpha-\\) hydrogen does not occur and hence no haloform reaction.",
    "image": null
  },
